//! member: true
//!
//! std.net — blocking TCP over rows: bind a listener, accept or dial a
//! connection, read and write text on it, and close it exactly once.
//! Stability tier: core.
//!
//! **Errors are rows, never traps** (D30, API-CONVENTIONS §2's trap rule
//! and §14's restatement of it for the os tier): a refused connection, a
//! peer that finished, a deadline, bytes that are not text and every other
//! host failure are ORDINARY outcomes a caller handles with `else` or
//! propagates with `?`. Nothing here traps, and nothing returns a
//! sentinel. The vocabulary is the s39 builtin tier's, adopted verbatim so
//! that std adds no translation layer:
//!
//! - `refused` — nobody is listening at that address
//! - `timeout` — the operation's deadline expired. Declared from sc08 so
//!   the vocabulary would be stable, REACHABLE since sc29: `set_deadline`
//!   / `set_listener_deadline` arm the budget (s106's `net_deadline`)
//! - `closed` — the peer finished: the socket's `eof`, an outcome rather
//!   than a failure, and the value that ends a read loop
//! - `utf8` — the bytes read are not well-formed UTF-8; a wolf `str` is
//!   UTF-8 by definition, so a read that cannot decode is a miss
//! - `io` — every other host failure, including a forged, closed or
//!   wrong-kind handle, coarsened to one tag because no caller can act
//!   differently on them yet (§12's rule 3)
//!
//! **Capability: `net` (I13).** Every function here except the two address
//! helpers reaches the host's network stack, is tagged `net` in the
//! toolchain's sandbox table, and is categorically refused at comptime
//! (D33) — sockets are the loudest case of that rule: `wolf add` must
//! never mean arbitrary code talks to the network with the builder's
//! credentials. The refusal is `E0701`, and
//! `tests/net/comptime_refuses.lu` holds it — one level down, at the builtin,
//! because a `comptime fn` cannot call across a module boundary at this pin
//! and so cannot reach this facade at all (F-0051).
//!
//! **Loopback and port 0, always — no external network, EVER.** Every
//! test and every doc example in this module binds `127.0.0.1:0`: the
//! host assigns an ephemeral port and the program learns it through
//! `port`. Nothing here names a fixed port, a foreign host or a name to
//! resolve. `connect` hands its address string to the host, which means a
//! hostname WOULD be resolved by the host resolver — std does not do it,
//! does not test it, and `dns`/`resolve` is not a function this module has
//! (see below).
//!
//! **Two operations sc08 withdrew are HERE now** (sc11): `read_all` and
//! `read_lines`, with `write_line` as the sending half that pairs with
//! them. All three need a loop that stops on `closed` and re-raises
//! `timeout`/`utf8`/`io` unchanged, which needs a handler that can say WHICH
//! tag fired — the shape that matched its first arm for every tag on the
//! executing lane through all of sc08 and sc09 (F-0052, closed at s70;
//! F-0043's payload half at s71). The version this module refused to ship
//! for two sprints would have treated a read error as the end of the stream
//! and handed back a truncated message with no indication, which is what
//! §14's rule about an indistinguishable failure mode exists to prevent.
//!
//! **The three-way split** (§8/§9's rule, applied to sockets). The
//! IMPLEMENTATION owns the builtins this module delegates to
//! (`net_listen`, `net_port`, `net_accept`, `net_connect`, `net_read`,
//! `net_write`, `net_read_bytes`, `net_write_bytes`, `net_deadline`,
//! `net_close`) — their semantics are therefore part of this
//! module's contract and are documented per function. std owns everything
//! else: the `Listener` and `Socket` values, the consuming closers, and
//! the pure address helpers. The LANGUAGE owns nothing here; there is no
//! socket syntax.
//!
//! **The SECOND address family lives in `std.net.unix` (sc36).** This
//! module is TCP over `"host:port"`; `[os.net.unix]`'s stream socket over
//! a filesystem PATH is `std.net.unix.listen`/`connect`, and it is a
//! separate module for §1's reason — wolf has no overloading, so `listen`
//! and `connect` cannot mean two things here. What that module returns is
//! one of THESE types: a unix listener is a `Listener`, a unix connection
//! is a `Socket`, and every function below serves them call for call
//! (`tests/net/unix/stream_surface.lu` measures the byte pair and both
//! deadlines over a socket path). Two functions behave differently on
//! one, and both say so in their own docs: `port` answers `io` because a
//! path has no port, and `close_listener` UNLINKS the path it bound.
//!
//! **Blocking, and what that means for a program.** Every operation blocks
//! the whole process: `accept` waits for a connection, `read` waits for
//! bytes. There is no reactor, no task parking and no `select` at this tier
//! — X6's completion-io posture is the s35 reactor's story and reaches std
//! when the builtin tier does. Two consequences a caller must design
//! around: a server written over these functions serves one connection at
//! a time, and an `accept` with no peer coming never returns UNLESS a
//! deadline is armed (`set_listener_deadline`, sc29 — before it, never
//! returns full stop, which is why every test in this module connects
//! BEFORE it accepts; the dial-first discipline stays, a deadline is a
//! net for a mistake rather than a license to block).
//!
//! **What is deliberately absent.** Each of these is a reviewed contract
//! with a filing behind it rather than an approximation:
//!
//! - ~~`set_deadline(mut s, millis)`~~ — **HERE now (sc29).** For nine
//!   sprints the `timeout` tag was declared and unreachable (F-0049: the
//!   runtime held a per-socket deadline no builtin exposed); s106's
//!   `net_deadline` builtin (#45's builtin half) closed the gap, and
//!   `set_deadline` below wraps it on ALL THREE lanes — lupin 0.1.18
//!   arms it too, measured. The pure-addition promise this bullet made
//!   was kept: no signature changed, the tag the vocabulary carried from
//!   day one is simply reachable at last, and
//!   `tests/net/read_deadline_row.lu` witnesses it firing.
//! - `read_line(mut s) -> str ! {closed, timeout, utf8, io}` — ONE line,
//!   as it arrives. Still blocked, and this is now the module's only
//!   blocked read: a buffered reader is writable in principle (a `str`
//!   field mutated through a `mut` parameter works on both executing lanes,
//!   measured in sc08) but FILLING the buffer is a byte-count read, and a
//!   chunk that ends inside a code point is a `utf8` miss whose bytes are
//!   already gone (F-0050). A line reader that corrupts the moment a stream
//!   carries a non-ASCII character is not a partial function, it is a broken
//!   one; §9's "refusal over approximation" decides it. `read_lines` below
//!   is NOT a substitute — it returns nothing until the peer closes — and
//!   the difference is stated on both.
//! - `write_all` — **not shipped because `write` already is one.** The
//!   builtin writes the whole `str` or raises; a short write is not
//!   observable, so a `write_all` loop here would be ceremony documenting
//!   a hazard that does not exist at this pin. The name is reserved for the
//!   day a partial-write surface appears, and `write`'s doc states the
//!   completeness it relies on.
//! - `shutdown(mut s, half)` — an orderly half-close. No builtin, so a
//!   writer cannot say "I am done sending" without closing the whole
//!   socket (F-0049).
//! - `peer_address` / `local_address` — `port` is the only address
//!   information the builtin tier reports, and it reports it as an `int`
//!   for the LOCAL end (F-0049).
//! - UDP (`send_to`/`recv_from`), name resolution, and any HTTP: no
//!   builtin surface at this pin, and the sprint's non-targets besides.
//!   TLS is never this tier's business.
//! - ~~Byte-level `read_bytes`/`write_bytes`~~ — **HERE now (sc29).**
//!   F-0080 predicted the fix's exact shape from the fs tier's (s90) and
//!   s106 shipped it: `net_read_bytes`/`net_write_bytes` carry
//!   `List[byte]` with no `utf8` row anywhere — a lone `0x80` is data —
//!   and the wrappers below adopt them verbatim. This is what makes a
//!   BINARY protocol over TCP writable at all (`std.x.tls.client` is the
//!   first consumer: a TLS record is not text). The one lane cost is
//!   lupin's: 0.1.18's is18 socket tier is the STR calls only, the byte
//!   pair does not resolve there (F-0102, filed), so the byte functions
//!   are compiler-lanes-only where the rest of the module is three-lane.
//!   `read` keeps its `utf8` row for the callers that want text.
//!
//! **Lanes (re-measured at the sc29 pin, wolf v0.2.0 + lupin 0.1.18).**
//! The str tier and `set_deadline` run on ALL THREE lanes now: the
//! checked tier since s39, the native rung since s106 crossed net (the
//! "checked lane only at s39" refusal this paragraph used to record is
//! history), and lupin since 0.1.18's is18 sockets (nonblocking polls
//! under the baton). The byte tier (`read_bytes`/`write_bytes`) is the
//! exception: both compiler rungs run it, lupin does not resolve it
//! (F-0102) — a program that reaches it there is an honest `unsupported`
//! at resolve, and because lupin resolves module bodies LAZILY the rest
//! of the module keeps its lane. Comptime refuses the whole capability
//! everywhere (D33).

/// A bound listener: the host socket this process accepts connections on.
///
/// TCP when `listen` made it, unix-domain when `std.net.unix.listen` did,
/// and nothing in the value records which — the operations are identical
/// once the socket exists, which is why there is one type (sc36). The two
/// places the family shows through are `port` and `close_listener`, and
/// both are documented where they are.
///
/// The field is an `int` because that is what the builtin tier hands out —
/// a small non-negative index into the machine's socket table, never a
/// pointer. Reading it is legal; passing a hand-built `Listener` to
/// `accept` is not a trap but a miss (`io`), because a forged descriptor is
/// a checkable condition rather than a contract violation, and a public
/// struct's field cannot be private at this pin.
///
/// **Staleness is prevented by the mode system**, exactly as it is for
/// `std.fs.File`: `close_listener` takes its `Listener` by `take` (X1), so
/// using one after closing it is a static use-after-move rather than a
/// row on every operation for a condition the compiler can see.
pub struct Listener {
    fd: int,
}

/// A connected socket: one end of an established stream, whichever side
/// opened it and whichever address family carried it.
///
/// `accept`, `connect` and `std.net.unix.connect` all answer one of these,
/// and nothing about the value records which — the operations are
/// identical once the connection exists, which is the whole point of the
/// type. No function here behaves differently on a unix-domain stream:
/// `close` never touches a path, because the LISTENER owns the name. The field is an `int` for
/// the reasons `Listener`'s is, and `close` consumes the value for the same
/// reason `close_listener` does.
pub struct Socket {
    fd: int,
}

/// Bind a listening socket to `addr` and return it.
///
/// `addr` is `"host:port"` as the host understands it — write
/// `"127.0.0.1:0"` and let the host choose the port, which is what `port`
/// below is for. Rows: `io` when the address cannot be bound at all
/// (already in use, not a local address, malformed), coarsened to one tag
/// because the builtin tier reports one. Never traps.
///
/// The listener starts accepting immediately: a peer may connect before
/// `accept` is ever called and waits in the host's backlog, which is what
/// makes a single-process test deterministic.
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_listen`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen(net.loopback(0))?
/// (net.port(srv) else 0) > 0
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn listen(addr: str) -> Listener ! {io} {
    Listener { fd: net_listen(addr)? }
}

/// The port `l` is bound to.
///
/// This is how a program learns the port the host chose for it after
/// `listen(":0")` — the port-0 discipline every test in this module
/// follows. Rows: `io` when the handle is closed, forged or the host
/// cannot report the address. Never traps.
///
/// Read mode: asking a listener its port does not change it. The answer is
/// the LOCAL port only; there is no peer or full-address accessor at this
/// pin (see the module header).
///
/// **On a `std.net.unix` listener this is `io`, always** — a path has no
/// port, and the `io` row is the builtin tier's own answer rather than a
/// check std adds (`[os.net.unix]`, measured on all three lanes and
/// pinned in `tests/net/unix/echo.lu`). A program that binds either
/// family and reports its address asks `port` and falls back to the path
/// it passed.
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_port`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv) else 0
/// p > 0
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn port(l: Listener) -> int ! {io} {
    net_port(l.fd)?
}

/// Take the next connection from `l`'s backlog, waiting until one arrives.
///
/// Rows: `timeout` when an armed deadline expires — arm one with
/// `set_listener_deadline` (reachable since sc29; it was declared-only
/// under F-0049 for nine sprints) — and `io` when the handle is closed,
/// forged or the host failed. Never traps.
///
/// **This parks until a connection is yours, and an armed deadline BOUNDS
/// the park** (`[os.net.accept]`, wolf-lang#242). With a budget armed by
/// `set_listener_deadline`, the call returns inside it — the connection,
/// or `timeout`. With no budget armed it parks indefinitely, in the
/// runtime's own wait rather than the kernel's, so a program that accepts
/// before anyone dials still waits; the dial-first test discipline stays
/// and the doc example below keeps it.
///
/// **To ask WITHOUT committing to the wait, use `net.wait`** — thirty
/// lines down this file, `[os.net.wait]`. A loop that multiplexes this
/// listener with its open connections asks `wait` for the ready set and
/// calls `accept` only when the answer names `l.fd`. That is the shape
/// `wait` exists for, and it is the answer to "can I poll a listener".
///
/// **A readiness wake is not a claim, and a `timeout` here can mean
/// someone else took it.** On a listener several processes share — the
/// prefork arrangement `[os.proc.inherit]` invites, and the one `wait`'s
/// clause points at — one arriving connection wakes EVERY hand and
/// exactly one of them takes it. A hand that loses the race finds nothing
/// there and waits again **against the same budget**, the one computed
/// when its call began and never a fresh one, so a lost race costs at
/// most the budget it already armed. What it eventually answers is
/// `timeout`, and on N hands that is the NORMAL outcome for N-1 of them:
/// not a failure to report, not a state change, just the connection this
/// hand did not get. A serving loop treats it as "go round again". This
/// is why a shared-listener hand arms a SHORT budget: there, a deadline
/// is the mechanism and not merely the net for a mistake that
/// `set_listener_deadline`'s own clause describes.
///
/// The accepted socket is BLOCKING whatever posture the listener holds,
/// so nothing about `read`/`write` changes.
///
/// `mut l` because accepting ADVANCES the listener: the connection this
/// call takes is one the next call will not see, which is state the value
/// names even though its `fd` field does not change — the same rule
/// `std.fs.read` follows for a file's position (§14).
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_accept`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv)?
/// var cli = net.connect("127.0.0.1:{p}")?
/// var conn = net.accept(mut srv)?
/// net.write(mut cli, "hi")?
/// (net.read(mut conn, 8) else "?") == "hi"
/// net.close(take cli)?
/// net.close(take conn)?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn accept(mut l: Listener) -> Socket ! {timeout, io} {
    Socket { fd: net_accept(l.fd)? }
}

/// Dial `addr` and return the connected socket.
///
/// Rows: `refused` when nobody is listening there — the commonest network
/// outcome there is, and a row rather than a trap because the caller could
/// not have checked; `timeout` when a deadline expires (a CONNECT
/// deadline has no arming surface here — `set_deadline` needs the
/// `Socket` this call has not yet produced, so on this function the tag
/// is still vocabulary-only); `io` for every other host failure,
/// including an address that does not parse. Never traps.
///
/// **This blocks until the connection completes or fails**, and the host
/// decides how long that takes. `addr` is handed to the host as written,
/// so a NAME would be resolved by the host resolver — std neither resolves
/// nor tests names, and every example here is loopback (see the module
/// header on the no-external-network law).
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to
/// `net_connect`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv)?
/// var cli = net.connect(net.loopback(p))?
/// net.close(take cli)?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn connect(addr: str) -> Socket ! {refused, timeout, io} {
    Socket { fd: net_connect(addr)? }
}

/// Read up to `max` bytes from `s`, decoded as UTF-8, waiting for at least
/// one byte.
///
/// Rows: `closed` when the peer finished — the socket's `eof`, an OUTCOME
/// that ends a read loop rather than a failure, and the tag a reader stops
/// on rather than reports; `timeout` when an armed deadline expires
/// (`set_deadline` — reachable since sc29); `utf8` when the bytes read are not well-formed UTF-8, WHICH
/// INCLUDES a chunk boundary that lands inside a code point, and the bytes
/// are consumed either way; `io` when the handle is closed, forged or the
/// host failed. Never traps.
///
/// **A byte-count read of text is not safe in general**, exactly as
/// `std.fs.read` is not: TCP delivers whatever has arrived, so a stream
/// carrying non-ASCII text can split a code point across two reads and the
/// second byte-sequence is a `utf8` miss. ASCII is safe at any size, and
/// the honest fix is byte-level io (F-0080, and the fs tier has had it
/// since s90 — `fs.read_chunk` is the shape this one wants), filed rather
/// than hidden. `max` at or below zero reads nothing and answers the empty
/// string without touching the socket.
///
/// `mut s` because reading CONSUMES from the stream: the bytes this call
/// takes are bytes the next call will not see (§14).
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_read`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv)?
/// var cli = net.connect("127.0.0.1:{p}")?
/// net.write(mut cli, "wolfpack")?
/// var conn = net.accept(mut srv)?
/// (net.read(mut conn, 4) else "?") == "wolf"
/// (net.read(mut conn, 4) else "?") == "pack"
/// net.close(take cli)?
/// net.close(take conn)?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn read(mut s: Socket, max: int) -> str ! {closed, timeout, utf8, io} {
    net_read(s.fd, max)?
}

/// Write all of `text` to `s`.
///
/// Rows: `closed` when the peer has gone away (a write to a finished
/// connection is the row, never a trap), `io` when the handle is closed,
/// forged or the host failed. Never traps, and there is no `utf8` row: a
/// `str` is already well-formed UTF-8.
///
/// **The whole string is written or the call raises** — the builtin loops
/// internally, so a partial write is not an outcome this surface has, and
/// that is why there is no `write_all` beside this function (see the
/// module header). What it does NOT promise is delivery: bytes handed to
/// the host may still be in flight, and only the peer's own read proves
/// otherwise.
///
/// Writes are unbuffered: every call reaches the host, so a program
/// writing a message in small pieces pays for each one.
///
/// `mut s` because writing advances the stream, the same rule `read`
/// follows (§14).
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_write`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv)?
/// var cli = net.connect("127.0.0.1:{p}")?
/// net.write(mut cli, "one ")?
/// net.write(mut cli, "two")?
/// net.close(take cli)?
/// var conn = net.accept(mut srv)?
/// (net.read(mut conn, 7) else "?") == "one two"
/// net.close(take conn)?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn write(mut s: Socket, text: str) -> () ! {closed, io} {
    net_write(s.fd, text)?
}

/// Read up to `max` BYTES from `s` as a `List[byte]` — the binary read.
///
/// Bytes are bytes: there is no `utf8` row here and can never be one
/// (the fs byte tier's posture, adopted verbatim per F-0080's ask —
/// a lone `0x80` is data, not a miss). This is the read a binary
/// protocol builds on; `read` above is the one for text.
///
/// Rows: `closed` when the peer finished (the row that ends a read
/// loop); `timeout` when an armed deadline expired (`set_deadline`);
/// `io` for a host failure or a bad handle. Never traps.
///
/// **A short read is normal**: up to `max` means "what the host had",
/// exactly as `read`'s doc states for text — a caller that needs N
/// bytes loops until it has them (`std.x.tls.client.read_exact` is the
/// worked example).
///
/// `mut s` because reading consumes from the stream (§14).
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to
/// `net_read_bytes` (s106) — both compiler lanes; lupin 0.1.18 does
/// not resolve the byte tier (F-0102), so this example's lupin lane is
/// an honest `unsupported`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv)?
/// var cli = net.connect("127.0.0.1:{p}")?
/// var msg = List[byte]()
/// (mut msg).push(0 as byte)
/// (mut msg).push(255 as byte)
/// net.write_bytes(mut cli, msg)?
/// var conn = net.accept(mut srv)?
/// let got = net.read_bytes(mut conn, 8) else List[byte]()
/// got.len == 2
/// net.close(take cli)?
/// net.close(take conn)?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn read_bytes(mut s: Socket, max: int) -> List[byte] ! {closed, timeout, io} {
    net_read_bytes(s.fd, max)?
}

/// Write all of `b` — raw bytes — to `s`.
///
/// The binary twin of `write`, and the same completeness promise: the
/// whole list is written or the call raises, so there is no partial
/// write to observe.
///
/// Rows: `closed` when the peer has gone away; **`invalid` — declared
/// and UNREACHABLE from typed code since sc35**, the same tag and the
/// same posture as `fs.write_bytes` (§14's os-tier vocabulary, adopted
/// verbatim). It named an element of `b` outside 0..255, rejected whole
/// before anything reached the wire; `b` is a `List[byte]` now and
/// cannot hold one, and the tag stays because the vocabulary is the
/// toolchain's (wolf-lang#231 kept `invalid` on `net_write_bytes` for
/// the FFI caller it is still true of). `io` for a host failure or a
/// bad handle. Never traps.
///
/// `mut s` because writing advances the stream (§14).
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to
/// `net_write_bytes` (s106) — both compiler lanes; lupin 0.1.18 does
/// not resolve the byte tier (F-0102).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv)?
/// var cli = net.connect("127.0.0.1:{p}")?
/// var msg = List[byte]()
/// (mut msg).push(128 as byte)
/// net.write_bytes(mut cli, msg)?
/// net.close(take cli)?
/// var conn = net.accept(mut srv)?
/// let got = net.read_bytes(mut conn, 8) else List[byte]()
/// got.len == 1
/// net.close(take conn)?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn write_bytes(mut s: Socket, b: List[byte]) -> () ! {closed, invalid, io} {
    net_write_bytes(s.fd, b)?
}

/// Arm (`ms > 0`) or clear (`ms <= 0`) a deadline BUDGET on `s`: every
/// subsequent parking operation on this socket — `read`, `read_bytes`,
/// `write` — resolves as its row's `timeout` tag when the budget fires
/// first.
///
/// **This is what makes `timeout` reachable** — the tag every read and
/// write signature has carried since sc08, declared against exactly
/// this day (F-0049's deadline half, closed by s106's `net_deadline`).
/// A socket with no deadline armed behaves as before: it blocks until
/// the host answers. The budget is per-OPERATION arming, not a
/// wall-clock alarm — it is consulted by each parking call, and
/// clearing it returns the socket to plain blocking.
///
/// Rows: `io` — arming fails only on a forged, closed or wrong-kind
/// handle (or a host that cannot hold a deadline). Never traps.
///
/// `mut s` because the socket's host state changes even though the
/// value's field does not (§14's stream rule: the call site spells the
/// mutation).
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to
/// `net_deadline` (s106) — ALL THREE lanes, lupin 0.1.18 included
/// (measured: an armed 200ms budget resolves a silent-peer read as
/// `timeout` there too).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv)?
/// var cli = net.connect("127.0.0.1:{p}")?
/// net.set_deadline(mut cli, 150)?
/// let missed = net.read(mut cli, 8) else "deadline"
/// missed == "deadline"
/// net.close(take cli)?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn set_deadline(mut s: Socket, ms: int) -> () ! {io} {
    net_deadline(s.fd, ms)?
}

/// Arm (`ms > 0`) or clear (`ms <= 0`) a deadline budget on the
/// LISTENER `l`: an `accept` whose peer never dials resolves `timeout`
/// instead of blocking forever.
///
/// **Why the name is not `set_deadline`**: `close_listener`'s reason —
/// no overloading, one verb per type, and the vocabulary word goes to
/// the connected socket (§1). This is the deadline that retires the
/// "an accept with no peer coming never returns" hazard the module
/// header documented from sc08 on; the dial-first test discipline
/// stays (a deadline is a net for a mistake, not a license to block).
///
/// **On a listener several processes accept from, it is the mechanism
/// rather than the net.** `[os.net.accept]` bounds a lost race by the
/// budget armed when the call began, so a prefork hand arms a SHORT one
/// deliberately: that budget is the most a connection taken by a sibling
/// can cost it before it goes round its loop again. See `accept`'s clause
/// for what the `timeout` means there.
///
/// Rows: `io` — a forged, closed or wrong-kind handle. Never traps.
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to
/// `net_deadline` (s106) — all three lanes.
///
/// Example (prose, because `accept`'s success type is a `Socket` no
/// deadline-firing example can produce — the fenced form would need a
/// diverging handler around a value the block must still type, F-0040's
/// monomorphic edge in one line): arm
/// `net.set_listener_deadline(mut srv, 150)?` and an `accept` with no
/// peer dialing resolves its `timeout` tag inside ~150ms instead of
/// blocking forever. `tests/net/accept_deadline_row.lu` witnesses the
/// tag riding out of `main`.
pub fn set_listener_deadline(mut l: Listener, ms: int) -> () ! {io} {
    net_deadline(l.fd, ms)?
}

/// Close `s`, consuming it.
///
/// Rows: `io` when the handle was already closed or is foreign. Never
/// traps.
///
/// `take` is the mode of the operation's meaning (§3, and §14's handle
/// rule): closing ends the socket's life, so the caller spells
/// `net.close(take s)` and the compiler rejects every later use as a
/// use-after-move. That is the whole staleness story for sockets, it costs
/// nothing at run time, and `tests/net/use_after_close.lu` holds it as a
/// rejection so it cannot rot.
///
/// Closing is also what tells the PEER the stream is finished: it is the
/// only half-close this tier has, so it is what makes the other end's next
/// read answer `closed`. A writer that wants to say "done sending" and keep
/// reading wants `shutdown`, which is a contract at this pin (F-0049).
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_close`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv)?
/// var cli = net.connect("127.0.0.1:{p}")?
/// net.close(take cli)?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn close(take s: Socket) -> () ! {io} {
    net_close(s.fd)?
}

/// Close the listener `l`, consuming it.
///
/// Rows: `io` when it was already closed or is foreign. Never traps.
///
/// **Why the name is not `close`.** wolf has no overloading, so one module
/// cannot spell one verb for two types (§1: one concept, one name — and
/// the vocabulary word `close` goes to the value a program handles most,
/// the connected socket). Closing a listener stops new connections; the
/// ones already accepted are separate sockets and are unaffected.
///
/// `take` for `close`'s reason: the handle is gone afterwards, and using
/// it is a static use-after-move.
///
/// **On a `std.net.unix` listener this call also UNLINKS the socket
/// path** — the runtime made the file, so the runtime removes it. Nothing
/// about the signature changes and no row is added: the unlink is best
/// effort, so a path that has already been moved or removed still closes
/// clean (`tests/net/unix/cleanup.lu` and `refused_row.lu`). A TCP
/// listener has no name and this sentence does not apply to one.
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_close`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn close_listener(take l: Listener) -> () ! {io} {
    net_close(l.fd)?
}

/// Every byte the peer sends until it closes, decoded as UTF-8, as one
/// `str`.
///
/// Rows: `timeout` when an armed deadline expires (`set_deadline` —
/// reachable since sc29, and on THIS function it turns a peer that stops
/// sending without closing from a hang into a row); `utf8` when what
/// arrived is not well-formed UTF-8, WHICH
/// INCLUDES a chunk boundary that lands inside a code point (see below);
/// `io` for a host failure or a bad handle. All three are RE-RAISED
/// unchanged from the read they came from. `closed` is not in the row: the
/// peer finishing is what makes this function RETURN, which is the whole
/// difference between reading everything and failing to read something.
/// Never traps.
///
/// **This blocks until the peer CLOSES**, not until it stops sending. A
/// peer that writes a request and waits for a reply is a peer this function
/// never returns from — so it is the right operation for a stream whose end
/// is the message boundary (a one-shot response, a file transfer) and the
/// wrong one for a conversation. `read` with a limit is the operation for a
/// conversation.
///
/// **The `utf8` hazard is inherited and is real**: the socket is read in
/// 4096-byte chunks and a chunk boundary can split a code point, so a
/// stream carrying non-ASCII text can raise `utf8` even though every byte
/// it sent was valid (F-0050 — the byte-level read that would fix it does
/// not exist). ASCII is safe at any size. The bytes read before the miss
/// are gone either way, which is why this function reports the tag rather
/// than returning a partial answer beside it.
///
/// **Why this function exists only now.** It was written and withdrawn
/// inside sc08: the loop must stop on `closed` and re-raise the other three
/// unchanged, and no handler could tell tags apart — a bare binding catches
/// everything (F-0043) and a `match` inside a handler matched its FIRST ARM
/// for every tag on the executing lane (F-0052). Both are closed at this
/// pin (s70's match tier, s71's payload pattern), so the handler below
/// discriminates on every lane. The version std refused to ship is visible
/// in what it would have done: treated a read error as the end of the
/// stream and handed the caller a truncated message with no indication
/// (§14's rule about a failure mode a module cannot distinguish).
///
/// `mut s` because reading consumes from the stream (§14).
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_read`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv)?
/// var cli = net.connect("127.0.0.1:{p}")?
/// net.write(mut cli, "the whole message")?
/// net.close(take cli)?
/// var conn = net.accept(mut srv)?
/// (net.read_all(mut conn) else "?") == "the whole message"
/// net.close(take conn)?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn read_all(mut s: Socket) -> str ! {timeout, utf8, io} {
    var out = ""
    var more = true
    while more {
        let chunk = net_read(s.fd, 4096) else |e| match e {
            closed => {
                more = false
                ""
            },
            timeout => {
                return timeout
            },
            utf8 => {
                return utf8
            },
            io => {
                return io
            },
        }
        if more {
            out += chunk
        }
    }
    out
}

/// Write `text` to `s` followed by exactly one `"\n"`.
///
/// Rows: `write`'s exactly — `closed` when the peer has gone away, `io` for
/// a host failure or a bad handle. Never traps, and there is no `utf8` row:
/// a `str` is already well-formed UTF-8.
///
/// This is the SENDING half of a line protocol, and it is one call rather
/// than two so that a message and its terminator cannot be separated by an
/// early return between them. The terminator is one `"\n"` on every
/// platform — no `"\r\n"` translation anywhere, matching `std.io`'s
/// writers, because a protocol that wants the other terminator can write it
/// with `write` and a program should never have to guess which one it got.
///
/// `mut s` because writing advances the stream (§14).
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_write`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv)?
/// var cli = net.connect("127.0.0.1:{p}")?
/// net.write_line(mut cli, "GET /")?
/// net.close(take cli)?
/// var conn = net.accept(mut srv)?
/// (net.read_all(mut conn) else "?") == "GET /\n"
/// net.close(take conn)?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn write_line(mut s: Socket, text: str) -> () ! {closed, io} {
    net_write(s.fd, "{text}\n")?
}

/// Every line the peer sends until it closes, terminators stripped.
///
/// Rows: `read_all`'s exactly — `timeout` (an armed deadline,
/// `set_deadline`), `utf8` (including a chunk boundary inside a code point), `io` —
/// and `closed` is again the OUTCOME that ends the read rather than a tag.
/// Never traps. Allocates the list.
///
/// This is the RECEIVING half of a line protocol, and its shape is the
/// honest one available at this pin: read the whole stream, then split it.
/// The alternative — a `read_line` that returns ONE line as it arrives —
/// needs a buffer filled by a byte-count read, and a chunk that ends inside
/// a code point loses those bytes with no way to report which line they
/// belonged to (F-0050). That function is a reviewed contract in the module
/// header, and this one is not a substitute for it: a caller who must react
/// to each line as it arrives cannot use this, because nothing is returned
/// until the peer closes.
///
/// A trailing terminator produces no final empty line (`"a\nb\n"` is two
/// lines, not three), the empty stream is an empty list, and the split is
/// `std.str.lines`'s — Unicode line boundaries, which is the builtin's
/// definition and therefore part of this contract.
///
/// `mut s` because reading consumes from the stream (§14).
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_read`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv)?
/// var cli = net.connect("127.0.0.1:{p}")?
/// net.write_line(mut cli, "one")?
/// net.write_line(mut cli, "two")?
/// net.close(take cli)?
/// var conn = net.accept(mut srv)?
/// let got = net.read_lines(mut conn) else List[str]()
/// got.len == 2
/// net.close(take conn)?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn read_lines(mut s: Socket) -> List[str] ! {timeout, utf8, io} {
    let whole = read_all(mut s)?
    whole.lines()
}

/// `host` and `port_number` joined as the address string `listen` and
/// `connect` take.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps — and pure, so unlike everything above it is
/// comptime-safe and reaches no host, which is why it is the one function
/// here a `comptime fn` may call. An IPv6 host (one containing a `:`) is
/// bracketed, because that is what makes `"::1"` and a port
/// unambiguous; anything else is joined with a single `:`. Nothing is
/// validated: an address the host cannot parse comes back as an `io` row
/// from `listen` or `connect`, where the host is the authority.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// net.endpoint("127.0.0.1", 8080) == "127.0.0.1:8080"
/// net.endpoint("::1", 8080) == "[::1]:8080"
/// net.endpoint("localhost", 0) == "localhost:0"
/// ```
pub fn endpoint(host: str, port_number: int) -> str {
    if host.contains(":") { return "[{host}]:{port_number}" }
    "{host}:{port_number}"
}

/// The loopback address with `port_number` — `endpoint("127.0.0.1", n)`.
///
/// Total, pure, comptime-safe. It exists to make the discipline this
/// module tests itself under writable in one call: `loopback(0)` asks the
/// host for an ephemeral port on the interface that cannot leave the
/// machine, which is the only address a std test or doc example is ever
/// allowed to name.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// net.loopback(0) == "127.0.0.1:0"
/// net.loopback(8080) == "127.0.0.1:8080"
/// ```
pub fn loopback(port_number: int) -> str {
    endpoint("127.0.0.1", port_number)
}

/// The options `listen_with` takes: what a prefork server needs and
/// nothing else.
///
/// Two fields, both public and both meaningful on their own, so a caller
/// writes the literal directly — `net.ListenOpts { reuse_port: true,
/// backlog: 128 }` — or starts from `listen_opts()` below and edits. There
/// is no builder here because there are two options; a `Command`-shaped
/// builder for two booleans-and-an-int would be ceremony (§1).
///
/// `reuse_port` sets `SO_REUSEPORT` **before** the bind. That "before" is
/// why this is a call and not a post-bind setter on a `Listener`: after
/// the bind the option is too late, and a setter that silently did
/// nothing is the API this shape exists to avoid.
///
/// `backlog` is the `listen(2)` queue hint. `<= 0` asks for the
/// runtime's default, which is 128 — std's own number, stated here so a
/// caller who wants it can say so explicitly rather than by omission.
/// The host clamps any hint to its own `somaxconn`, so this is a hint in
/// the honest sense: you may ask, the kernel decides.
pub struct ListenOpts {
    reuse_port: bool,
    backlog: int,
}

/// The options that make `listen_with` behave exactly like `listen`.
///
/// `listen_with(addr, listen_opts())` is `listen(addr)` call for call —
/// the clause says so of the builtin (`net_listen_with(addr, false, 0)`
/// is `net_listen(addr)`), and this is that sentence made reachable. Use
/// it as the base you edit one field of.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps. Pure and comptime-safe — building options
/// binds nothing.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let o = net.listen_opts()
/// o.reuse_port == false
/// o.backlog == 0
/// ```
pub fn listen_opts() -> ListenOpts {
    ListenOpts { reuse_port: false, backlog: 0 }
}

/// Bind a listening socket to `addr` with `opts` and return it.
///
/// **This is an ACQUISITION call and nothing else.** What it answers is
/// the ordinary `Listener` every other function in this module already
/// serves — `port`, `accept`, `set_listener_deadline`, `close_listener`
/// — so the surface does not grow by one function for one more way to
/// get a listener. That is the same rule `std.net.unix` follows and the
/// reason it needed only an address call.
///
/// **What `reuse_port` buys, and what it does NOT promise.** With the
/// option set, N listeners — in one process or across N processes — may
/// hold one address at once, every member of the group carrying the
/// option. What the KERNEL then does with the group is the host's, and
/// this module states it rather than promising it: **linux** distributes
/// accepts across the group by a 4-tuple hash, so every hand accepts;
/// **macOS** hands every SYN to the newest bound socket and falls to a
/// survivor when that socket closes — the port is shared and failover is
/// gap-free, but accepts are NOT distributed. A program that assumes
/// distribution is a program that works on one of this project's two
/// serving hosts. Both hosts guarantee exactly two things, and they are
/// the two a caller may build on: **every dial is accepted by SOME
/// member**, and **the survivor takes every dial after the others
/// close**. `tests/net/reuse_port.lu` is the assertion about precisely
/// those two and deliberately asserts nothing about which hand.
///
/// Rows, and this call has more of them than `listen` on purpose:
/// `unsupported` when THE HOST does not serve an option asked for —
/// windows, by name, for `reuse_port`, because its `SO_REUSEADDR` lets
/// any process take a bound port and then delivers every dial to the
/// first socket bound, so the second binds and serves nothing; that is
/// refused rather than aliased. `exists` when another socket already
/// holds the address without the option on both sides — the "already in
/// use" answer, spelled in the vocabulary `[os.net.unix]` gave this
/// family rather than a new tag. `denied` for a privileged port the
/// caller may not bind. `io` for the rest. Never traps.
///
/// **`listen`'s own row stays `{io}`** — its lowering coarsens — so a
/// program that wants the distinction calls this one even with default
/// options. That is a real reason to prefer `listen_with`, and the only
/// one.
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to
/// `net_listen_with`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen_with(net.loopback(0), net.listen_opts())?
/// (net.port(srv) else 0) > 0
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn listen_with(addr: str, opts: ListenOpts) -> Listener ! {unsupported, exists, denied, io} {
    Listener { fd: net_listen_with(addr, opts.reuse_port, opts.backlog)? }
}

/// Adopt a listening socket this process was HANDED rather than bound.
///
/// This is the child's half of descriptor inheritance: a parent spawns
/// with a set of listeners and the child receives them as descriptors
/// `3, 4, …` in the order given, so `adopt_listener(3)` is "the first
/// listener my parent handed me" and no number ever has to be told to
/// the child out of band. An ACQUISITION call, like `listen_with`: what
/// it answers is the ordinary `Listener`, so accept, both deadlines and
/// the closer come free.
///
/// **The runtime asks the KERNEL what the number is** rather than
/// trusting the caller: a stream socket with no peer, and, where the
/// kernel keeps a listening flag, that flag. Anything else — not a
/// socket, a connected stream, a family the table does not serve, a
/// number this process already adopted, a negative or absurd number — is
/// the `io` row. **Never a trap, and never a wrapped stranger**; a
/// forged descriptor is a checkable condition, which is the same
/// posture `Listener`'s own doc takes about a hand-built handle.
///
/// **Ownership at close, which is the sentence a prefork master needs.**
/// An adopted descriptor belongs to exactly one wolf handle, and
/// `close_listener` on that handle **does close the inherited fd**. The
/// adopter owns the number from the adopt on; a second adopt of a
/// still-live number is `io` rather than a double close waiting to
/// happen, and closing the handle releases the number so a freshly
/// handed one adopts again.
///
/// **What the adopter does NOT own is anything OUTSIDE the descriptor.**
/// An adopted `AF_UNIX` listener **does not unlink its socket file at
/// close**. `std.net.unix`'s cleanup posture belongs to the process that
/// BOUND the path — for an inherited listener, the parent. A child that
/// unlinked the path would delete the address its siblings are still
/// serving on, and this is the one place where an adopted listener and a
/// bound one behave differently at `close_listener`.
///
/// An adopted descriptor is marked close-on-exec on adoption, so this
/// process's own children inherit only what it hands them in turn.
///
/// Rows: `unsupported` where the host or the tier does not serve the
/// call, BY NAME — windows (a `SOCKET` is not a small stable number a
/// parent can name by position) and the CHECKED machine, which refuses
/// it because it is the `wolf` binary interpreting a program, so a
/// descriptor handed to "the program's child" would be handed to the
/// compiler's child. `io` for every failed check above. Never traps.
///
/// **There is no `std.process` half of this pair at this pin.** The
/// builtin `os_spawn_with` exists upstream; wrapping it is a
/// `std.process` change with its own `Command` question (whether a
/// `Command` grows an inherit set or the spawn takes one), and sc37
/// landed the acquisition half alone rather than guess at the builder.
/// Until it lands, the parent half of a prefork server is reachable only
/// through the builtin. Recorded as a residue, not a design.
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to
/// `net_adopt_listener`.
///
/// A prose example, because a doc example cannot spawn its own parent:
/// a child started by `os_spawn_with(exe, args, [srv])` reaches its
/// inherited listener with `let srv = net.adopt_listener(3)?` and from
/// there uses `net.accept` exactly as the binder would.
/// `tests/net/adopt_rows.lu` is the witness.
pub fn adopt_listener(fd: int) -> Listener ! {unsupported, io} {
    Listener { fd: net_adopt_listener(fd)? }
}

/// Which of `fds` can be read WITHOUT blocking, as a subset in the
/// caller's order.
///
/// Every other blocking call in this module waits on ONE socket. This is
/// the one that waits on many, and it is what a serving loop that spawns
/// no tasks has instead of a scheduler: without it a single-loop server
/// must time-slice — a short deadline on the listener, another on every
/// open connection, every idle pass — and spends its life waking up to
/// learn that nothing happened.
///
/// **An EMPTY answer is the deadline expiring with nothing ready. That
/// is an ANSWER, not a failure**, and a loop that treats it as one is
/// the mistake this sentence exists to prevent. `wait` returns a
/// possibly-empty `List[int]`; it does not raise when nothing is ready.
///
/// **It takes raw descriptors, not handles, and that is deliberate.** A
/// ready set mixes listeners and streams — that is the whole point of it
/// — and wolf has no sum type that could hold both at this pin. So the
/// argument is the `fd` field both `Listener` and `Socket` publish, and
/// the idiom is to build the list from them:
///
///     var set = List[int]()
///     (mut set).push(srv.fd)
///     (mut set).push(cli.fd)
///     let ready = net.wait(set, 50)?
///
/// The answer is a list of those same numbers, so a caller matches each
/// against the handle it came from. Reading `.fd` is legal by the
/// `Listener` doc's own rule; nothing here forges one.
///
/// **`deadline_ms`**: negative waits until something is ready, `0` asks
/// and returns at once, positive is milliseconds.
///
/// **Readiness is level-triggered**: asking consumes nothing, so two
/// waits with the same set answer the same until the program actually
/// reads or accepts. A stream whose peer has CLOSED is READY — the
/// `read` that follows is what reports `closed`, which is how a loop
/// learns to drop it.
///
/// Rows: `io` for a forged or closed handle anywhere in the set (nothing
/// is waited on), for an EMPTY set with an unbounded deadline (nothing
/// could ever end that wait — a program that means to sleep says
/// `time.sleep_ms`), and for a failure of the poll itself. An empty set
/// with a `0` or positive deadline is fine and answers empty. Never
/// traps.
///
/// The call does not interact with the task layer: it neither parks a
/// task nor disturbs the reactor's registrations, so a program may mix
/// it with `spawn` freely. Every tier-1 host answers this question and
/// every tier serves it.
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_wait`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen(net.loopback(0))?
/// var set = List[int]()
/// (mut set).push(srv.fd)
/// let ready = net.wait(set, 0)?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ready.len == 0
/// ```
pub fn wait(fds: List[int], deadline_ms: int) -> List[int] ! {io} {
    net_wait(fds, deadline_ms)?
}
